e two W4, could pair or use.\n\nBut better to preserve options.\n\nMoreover, claiming W2 exposes a meld, which may signal my suit, allowing others to block.\n\nCurrently, no one is showing much in W \u2014 previous player played W9, W7, W3, W1 \u2014 so some W out, but not many.\n\nUnshown W: plenty.\n\nBut still, better to stay concealed.\n\nAlso, I have honor singles \u2014 F2, F4 \u2014 could become pairs if I draw.\n\nBut low probability.\n\nAnother consideration: safety.\n\nIf I pass, I can draw and potentially win later.\n\nBut right now, I'm not in tenpai.\n\nAfter Chi, I'll be closer, but still not necessarily.\n\nLet\u2019s count tiles.\n\nAfter Chi W2, I form [W2,W3,W4], now my hand tiles: W5, T2, T4, B2, B5, F2, F4 \u2014 7 tiles, need to discard one.\n\nSay I discard T2.\n\nThen I have three melds? No: after Chi, I have two melds: [234] and [567], and 7 tiles \u2014 too many.\n\nNo: when you Chi, you add a meld (3 tiles), so you go from 13 tiles to 14 (after taking discard), then discard one, back to 13, with one more meld.\n\nCurrently: 10 hand tiles + 1 meld (3 tiles) = 13 physical tiles.\n\nAfter Chi: take W2, now 11 hand tiles? No.\n\nStandard: you have 13 tiles in hand before your turn.\n\nWhen someone discards, you can claim it to form a meld, using two from hand.\n\nSo you give up two hand tiles and gain a meld of three, including the discarded tile.\n\nNet: hand tiles decrease by 2, meld count increases by 1.\n\nIn this case:\n\nBefore: hand: 10 tiles, melds: 1\n\nAfter Chi W2: use W3 and W4 from hand, claim W2, form new meld [W2,W3,W4]\n\nNow hand: 10 - 2 = 8 tiles: W2, W5, T2, T4, B2, B5, F2, F4? Wait no \u2014 I used W3 and W4, but I had W2 in hand \u2014 still have W2.\n\nHand before: W2, W3, W4, W5, T2, T4, B2, B5, F2, F4\n\nUse W3 and W4 to form meld with discarded W2 \u2192 so remove W3, W4 from hand.\n\nAdd meld [W2,W3,W4]\n\nNow hand: W2, W5, T2, T4, B2, B5, F2, F4 \u2014 8 tiles.\n\nThen I must discard one of these 8.\n\nSo I end up with two melds and 8 hand tiles.\n\nStill far from winning.\n\nIf I pass, I keep 10 hand tiles, can draw a tile, then discard.\n\nMore flexibility.\n\nMoreover, by passing, I avoid exposing another meld, preserving chance for Fully Concealed Hand (4 points) or Concealed Hand (2 points).\n\nAlso, Self Drawn (1 point) possible.\n\nWhereas after Chi, I have two exposed melds, so no fully concealed.\n\nFurthermore, the tile W2 is not particularly rare \u2014 unshown 3, so not last tiles.\n\nNo Last Tile patterns.\n\nAlso, no immediate benefit.\n\nTherefore, passing is better.\n\nBut is there a defensive reason?\n\nThe previous player has a pung of T8 \u2014 possibly waiting for fourth or pair.\n\nBut T8 is not in my hand.\n\nOthers not threatening.\n\nSo overall, preserving flexibility is best.\n\nConclusion: Pass.\n\nAnswer: Pass"}
