ttern. Keeping B2 doesn't strongly contribute.\n\n3. **Play B5**: Could be part of [B4,B5,B6] or [B5,B6,B7]. But across player has [B5,B6,B7] already, so he has B5, B6, B7. He likely won\u2019t discard B5/B6. Also, B5 only one left unshown \u2014 likely already used. So [B4,B5,B6] is dangerous to pursue. Not promising.\n\n4. **Play T1**: Could form [T1,T2,T3]. But T2 and T3: many are out. Across player has [T3,T4,T5], next player has [T2,T3,T4] \u2014 so both have T2/T3. T1 only one left. So [T1,T2,T3] is unlikely. T1 is terminal, so keeping it hurts \u201cAll Simples\u201d but we\u2019re not going for that.\n\n5. **Play W4**: I already have [W2,W3,W4] and [W5,W6,W7]. W4 could link as middle for [W3,W4,W5], but I don\u2019t have W3 or W5 now. W4 is also part of upper/lower edge. But more critically, **I already used W4 in a meld**, and hold one in hand. Two W4s total. Remaining W4: 2 unshown \u2192 2 still available. But next player has [W4,W5,W6] \u2014 so they have W4 too. Likely others are cautious. Discarding W4 might be safe, but it breaks potential for [W3,W4,W5] type overlap.\n\nHowever, consider **Pure Double Chow**: If I had two identical chows, e.g., two [W2,W3,W4], I could score 1 point. But I only have one [W2,W3,W4], and one [W5,W6,W7], and one [T2,T3,T4]. No duplicates.\n\nBut notice: I have two chows in W: [W2,W3,W4] and [W5,W6,W7] \u2192 this is a **Short Straight** (two chows in same suit with successive numbers: 234 and 567 \u2192 gap at 4, not consecutive chows). But 234 and 567 are separated \u2014 not adjacent. So no Short Straight (which requires like 234+345 or 567+678). So no.\n\nWait \u2014 what about **Pure Shifted Chows**? Three successive chows in same suit with interval 1 or 2. I only have two W chows: 234 and 567 \u2192 interval of 1 (from 4 to 5), but missing 345 or 456. Not applicable.\n\nBest hope: **All Chows** (2 pts) + **No Honors** (1 pt) + possibly **Mixed Shifted Chows** or **Mixed Triple Chow**?\n\nBut I have three chows already. Need one more chow and a pair.\n\nCurrently, my hand has five singles. I need to draw a tile that forms a chow with two of them, or draws into a pair.\n\nBut for now, I must discard.\n\nBest discard is one that:\n- Minimizes damage\n- Maximizes future flexibility\n- Moves toward a coherent scoring pattern\n\nF4 is the only honor. Discarding it clears the way for **No Honors** and reduces useless tile.\n\nMoreover, opponents may be wary of giving me W or T tiles, but B is less developed.\n\nAcross player has B1 pung and B567 chow \u2014 so he may discard B4, B8, B9, etc.\n\nBut B2 and B5 in my hand \u2014 possible to form [B2,B3,B4] if I draw B3/B4.\n\nBut B3: only one left, B4: one left \u2014 risky.\n\nAlternatively, T1 could pair with another T1, but only one T1 left.\n\nW4: I have one in hand, two unshown. Could draw another W4 for pair? Possible.\n\nBut W4 is in high demand \u2014 two players have W chows.\n\nStill, among the options, **discarding F4** is safest because:\n- It\u2019s an honor, not contributing to chows\n- Doesn\u2019t block any chow formation\n- Helps achieve **No Honors**\n- Reduces chance of being stuck with singleton honor\n- F4 is prevalent wind, but without a pung, it's just 1 tile \u2014 useless alone\n\nOther discards (B2, B5, T1, W4) are suited and could potentially be part of future chows or pairs. F4 cannot form a chow.\n\nThus, F4 is the least useful tile.\n\nReason: F4 is the only honor tile in hand, and discarding it promotes a cleaner \"All Chows\" hand with potential for \"No Honors\", while preserving flexibility in the suited game. It has no chow potential and low value without a pair or pung.\n\nAnswer: Play F4"}
